David Parish","parties":["National Democratic Congress","New National Party"],"education":["University of the West Indies","University of Calgary","Presentation Brothers College"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"George Brizan"},"Q370110":{"id":"Q370110","name":"Keith Mitchell","desc":"Grenadian politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Keith_Mitchell.jpg/330px-Keith_Mitchell.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1946-11-12","died":null,"birthplace":"Saint George Parish","parties":["New National Party","Grenada Democratic Movement"],"education":["University of the West Indies","American University","Howard University","Presentation Brothers College"],"occupations":["mathematician","university teacher","politician","cricketer"],"wiki":"Keith Mitchell"},"Q57796":{"id":"Q57796","name":"Tillman Thomas","desc":"Grenadian Prime Minister","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Tillman_Thomas.jpg/330px-Tillman_Thomas.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1945-06-13","died":null,"birthplace":"Grenada","parties":["National Democratic Congress","Grenada Democratic Movement","New National Party"],"education":["Fordham University","University of the West Indies"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Tillman Thomas"},"Q112679881":{"id":"Q112679881","name":"Dickon Mitchell","desc":"Grenadian politician (1978-)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Prime_Minister_of_Grenada_Mr._Dickon_Mitchell.jpg/330px-Prime_Minister_of_Grenada_Mr._Dickon_Mitchell.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1978-10-08","died":null,"birthplace":"Saint David Parish","parties":["National Democratic Congress"],"education":["University of the West Indies at Cave Hill","Hugh Wooding Law School"],"occupations":["politician","lawyer"],"wiki":"Dickon Mitchell"},"Q43274":{"id":"Q43274","name":"Charles III","desc":"King of the United Kingdom since 2022 (born 1948)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/King_Charles_III_(July_2023).jpg/330px-King_Charles_III_(July_2023).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1948-11-14","died":null,"birthplace":"Buckingham Palace","parties":[],"education":["Aberystwyth University","Trinity College","Royal Air Force College Cranwell","Hill House International Junior School"],"occupations":["writer","polo player","entrepreneur","helicopter pilot"],"wiki":"Charles III"}},"summary":"Grenada is an archipelagic country of the West Indies in the eastern Caribbean Sea. The southernmost of the Windward Islands, Grenada is directly south of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines and about 160 kilometres (100 miles) north of Trinidad and the South American mainland.\nGrenada consists of the island of Grenada itself, two smaller islands, Carriacou and Petite Martinique, and several small islands that lie to the north of the main island and are a part of the Grenadines. Its size is 344 square kilometres (133 sq mi), with an estimated population of 117,362 as of 2026. Its capital is St. George's. Grenada is also known as the \"Island of Spice\" due to its production of nutmeg and mace.\nBefore the arrival of Europeans in the Americas, Grenada was inhabited by Indigenous peoples from South America called Kalinago. Christopher Columbus sighted Grenada in 1498 during his third voyage to the Americas. Following several unsuccessful attempts by Europeans to colonise the island, due to resistance from resident Kalinago people, French settlement and colonisation began in 1649 and continued for the next century. Between 1669 and 1808, according to the Voyages database, approximately 127,600 African slaves were brought to Grenada by the British and French.  On 10 February 1763, Grenada was ceded to the British under the Treaty of Paris. British rule continued until 1974 (except for a brief French takeover between 1779 and 1783). From 1958 to 1962, Grenada was part of the Federation of the West Indies, a short-lived federation of British West Indian colonies. On 3 March 1967, it was granted full autonomy over its internal affairs as an Associated State.\nIndependence was granted on 7 February 1974 under the leadership of Eric Gairy, who became the first prime minister of Grenada as a sovereign state. The new country became a member of the Commonwealth of Nations, with Queen Elizabeth II as head of state. In March 1979, the Marxist–Leninist New JEWEL Movement overthrew Gairy's government in a coup and established the People's Revolutionary Government (PRG), a de facto dictatorship headed by Maurice Bishop as prime minister. Bishop was later arrested and executed by members of the People's Revolutionary Army (PRA), which was followed by a U.S.-led invasion in October 1983. The current head of state is King Charles III, King of Grenada, who is represented by the Governor-General of Grenada.","history":"The history of Grenada in the Caribbean, part of the Lesser Antilles group of islands, covers a period from the earliest human settlements to the establishment of the contemporary nationstate of Grenada. First settled by indigenous peoples, Grenada by the time of European contact was inhabited by the Caribs. French colonists killed most of the Caribs on the island and established plantations on the island, eventually importing African slaves to work on the sugar plantations.\nControl of the island was disputed by Great Britain and France in the 18th century, with the British ultimately prevailing. In 1795, Fédon's Rebellion, inspired by the Haitian Revolution, very nearly succeeded, taking significant military intervention to quell. Slavery was abolished in 1833, and in 1885, the island's capital, St. George's, became the capital of the British Windward Islands.\nGrenada achieved independence from Britain in 1974. Following a coup by the Marxist New Jewel Movement in 1979, the island was invaded by United States troops and the government overthrown. The island's major crop, nutmeg, was significantly damaged by Hurricane Ivan in 2004.","historyTitle":"History of Grenada","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,94058],[1961,95335],[1962,96375],[1963,97195],[1964,97760],[1965,98067],[1966,98121],[1967,98066],[1968,98078],[1969,98204],[1970,98414],[1971,98648],[1972,98777],[1973,98709],[1974,98486],[1975,98126],[1976,97632],[1977,97017],[1978,96305],[1979,95490],[1980,94538],[1981,94052],[1982,94383],[1983,94948],[1984,95498],[1985,96111],[1986,96790],[1987,97526],[1988,98308],[1989,99121],[1990,99957],[1991,100793],[1992,101602],[1993,102382],[1994,103138],[1995,103869],[1996,104584],[1997,105293],[1998,106009],[1999,106727],[2000,107451],[2001,107987],[2002,108404],[2003,108884],[2004,109323],[2005,109734],[2006,110135],[2007,110522],[2008,110896],[2009,111254],[2010,111586],[2011,112079],[2012,112775],[2013,113487],[2014,114132],[2015,114676],[2016,115130],[2017,115438],[2018,115691],[2019,115981],[2020,116341],[2021,116688],[2022,116913],[2023,117081],[2024,117207],[2025,117303]],"gdp":[[1977,71494500],[1978,88322400],[1979,102244000],[1980,110900000],[1981,115652000],[1982,125436000],[1983,131804000],[1984,145533000],[1985,167728000],[1986,187590000],[1987,215010000],[1988,236358000],[1989,267328000],[1990,278099000],[1991,300758000],[1992,310160000],[1993,309812000],[1994,325112000],[1995,342173000],[1996,366911000],[1997,392191000],[1998,445904000],[1999,482009000],[2000,520044000],[2001,520444000],[2002,540337000],[2003,591018000],[2004,599119000],[2005,695556000],[2006,698701000],[2007,758684000],[2008,825976000],[2009,771276000],[2010,771015000],[2011,778656000],[2012,799881000],[2013,842619000],[2014,911496000],[2015,997007000],[2016,1061640000],[2017,1125690000],[2018,1166510000],[2019,1213490000],[2020,1043410000],[2021,1122220000],[2022,1224010000],[2023,1336420000],[2024,1351270000],[2025,1420170000]],"gdpPerCapita":[[1977,736.927],[1978,917.111],[1979,1070.73],[1980,1173.08],[1981,1229.66],[1982,1329.01],[1983,1388.17],[1984,1523.94],[1985,1745.15],[1986,1938.11],[1987,2204.64],[1988,2404.26],[1989,2696.98],